#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055780} :activity/id #uuid "a6abcd14-76de-59df-adaa-1dc632470883" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049986 :topic/id #uuid "2424a53c-3dea-5c8f-aeb6-365cf563d5f2" :topic/math-academy-id 38} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055780 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056020 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx + 4 < 7\n$$\n\nTo **solve** this inequality, we need to isolate the variable $x$ on one side.\n\nInequalities are just like equations because they can be solved using the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nSo, to solve this inequality, we subtract $4$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x + 4 & < 7 \\\\ x + 4 - 4 & < 7 - 4 \\\\ x & < 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe solution is $x < 3$. This tells us that any value of $x$ smaller than $3$ satisfies the original inequality.\n\nWe can represent the solution to this inequality using a number line as follows:\n\n![](images/55/556401d043fbd285a42b257e3c32f0a858377355674c264b90396e43ca8a8a13.png)\n\nLet's take a look at an inequality that we can solve using the multiplication principle." :tutorial/id #uuid "63426fbc-7f6c-516c-a3e9-9f1262862928" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8099 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5d1cc5da-4e34-51ae-843a-e59df07e8c64" :step/math-academy-id 52055 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055781}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055781 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054769 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054399 :question/id #uuid "121c55cc-404a-53e7-81f7-6edba34b0796" :question/math-academy-id "e-12956"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf8a6a4f-38ce-5ece-bdcb-6f2b58e2605c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055019}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a One-Step Inequality"} :step/id #uuid "9e6e8c20-c9b3-5288-8894-994f1e8693a4" :step/math-academy-id 52056 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055782}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055782 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056021 :tutorial/content "We've seen that solving linear inequalities is similar to solving linear equations.\n\nHowever, there is one additional rule that we need to remember when solving inequalities:\n\n> *When multiplying or dividing both sides by a **negative number**, we flip the inequality sign.*\n\nWhen we \"flip the inequality sign,\" we switch the direction of the inequality symbol. For example:\n\n- \"$\\color{blue}<$\" becomes \"$\\color{red}>$\" and vice versa\n- \"$\\color{blue}\\leq$\" becomes \"$\\color{red}\\geq$\" and vice versa\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-3x \\gt 9\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality using the multiplication principle. However, when we divide both sides by $-3$, we have to flip the inequality sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3x & >9 \\\\ \\frac{-3x}{-3} & <\\frac{9}{-3} \\\\ \\frac{-3x}{-3} & < - 3 \\\\ \\frac{-3x}{-3} & < - 3 \\\\ x & < - 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\lt -3$.\n\nWe'll discuss why we need to flip the inequality sign at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's get some practice at applying this idea." :tutorial/id #uuid "2c47a872-da72-51b3-ac92-ef955acc3f08" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8100 :tutorial/title "Multiplying or Dividing an Inequality by a Negative Number"} :step/id #uuid "cb7661d0-6da1-518d-9228-3db6e6b0cead" :step/math-academy-id 52059 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055783}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055783 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054770 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054400 :question/id #uuid "7424a287-2269-5044-abb7-1004b3826aca" :question/math-academy-id "e-12957"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "281841fb-287e-597a-abb1-3917212e0dcb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055201}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a One-Step Inequality When Flipping Is Required"} :step/id #uuid "18957d57-5792-5701-b6f4-614dc92db70f" :step/math-academy-id 52060 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055784}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055784 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054771 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054401 :question/id #uuid "82a9a75f-ab0e-54c3-9a0d-82c8497714cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-12958"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "200fc394-b299-5114-8f39-e6265f3c1166" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055025}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a One-Step Inequality With Fractions When Flipping Is Required"} :step/id #uuid "0607f740-0035-5986-a51a-77aa35a3ca25" :step/math-academy-id 52063 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055785}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055785 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054772 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054402 :question/id #uuid "1154ded3-0841-5358-980c-15c0f1841e82" :question/math-academy-id "e-12959"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6b0cad0-354b-5c4c-9fe2-3aceff7e5b6e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055023}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality and Adjusting the Final Result"} :step/id #uuid "351bf5fa-de4c-5dd3-b946-23f3767c42b1" :step/math-academy-id 52066 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055786}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055786 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056022 :tutorial/content "We'll now learn why we must flip the inequality sign when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number.\n\nTo help us, let's consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\n6 > 0\n$$\n\nThis is a true statement, and it remains true if we multiply or divide both sides by a positive number.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Multiplying the inequality by $2$ gives $12 > 0$, which is *true*.$\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- Dividing the inequality by $2$ gives $3 > 0$, which is also *true*. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nHowever, if we multiply or divide both sides by a negative number, *without* flipping the inequality sign, we get a false statement.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Multiplying the inequality by $-2$ *without flipping the inequality sign* gives $-12 > 0$, which is *false*. $\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- Dividing the inequality by $-2$ *without flipping the inequality sign* gives $-3 > 0$, which is also *false*. $\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo, whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we need to flip the sign of the inequality for it to remain true.\n\n- Multiplying the inequality by $-2$ *and flipping the inequality sign* gives $-12 < 0$, which is *true*. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- Dividing the inequality by $-2$ *and flipping the sign* gives $-3 < 0$, which is also *true*. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f9eacf1c-4f75-5dfe-a8e7-798404a6280d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8101 :tutorial/title "Why We Flip the Inequality Sign"} :step/id #uuid "e300a5cf-0bdb-5c23-9a03-3bb60a12a788" :step/math-academy-id 52070}] :activity/title "Solving One-Step Linear Inequalities" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054900}]}
